          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "**Please arrange a medical appointment soon.** A quick check of urgency returned \"routine,\" but that is not a clearance. Heaviness behind the breastbone that comes on when you walk uphill and eases when you stop is the classic description of stable angina in the reference material, and clinicians need to assess it. Don't wait for it to come up at a routine visit.\n\n**Call emergency services now** if you get any of these (from the chest pain guidance):\n- Pressure, tightness, squeezing or heaviness that lasts more than a few minutes, or goes away and comes back.\n- Pain or discomfort spreading to the arms, shoulders, neck, jaw, back or upper abdomen.\n- Chest discomfort with breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea, vomiting, light-headedness, or a sense of impending doom.\n- Chest discomfort at rest, or brought on by less effort than usual.\n\nThe same guidance says any chest discomfort in someone with known coronary artery disease, diabetes, or several cardiovascular risk factors should be taken just as seriously.\n\n**What it could be.** The two main possibilities to raise with a clinician are stable angina from coronary artery disease and acid reflux (GERD). Reflux usually causes a burning feeling linked to meals, bending forward or lying down, and your pattern tied to walking uphill fits it less well. The sources do not establish which, if either, applies to you. Only an assessment can do that.\n\n**Prevention, if it turns out to be angina.** The secondary prevention guidance applies to people who already have established coronary artery disease, so it describes what would be considered after a diagnosis, not something to start on your own now. It describes a combination of measures, since no single one carries the benefit:\n- Long-term single antiplatelet treatment for most people.\n- High-intensity statin treatment for established atherosclerotic disease.\n- A blood pressure target below 130/80 mm Hg for most people.\n- Other drugs depending on the person, such as certain blood-pressure drugs where diabetes, reduced heart function or kidney disease is present, and beta-blockers after a heart attack.\n- Cardiac rehabilitation, a supervised exercise and education programme.\n- Smoking cessation, described as the largest single modifiable factor.\n- Diabetes treatment, if present, favouring agents with proven heart benefit.\n- Influenza vaccination.\n\nThe sources don't name the medicines used to control angina symptoms, so your clinician will need to explain those options. Another source covers general lifestyle measures for blood pressure, such as reducing salt, eating more vegetables, fruit and whole grains, regular activity, managing weight, limiting alcohol and stopping smoking. That source is about hypertension, not angina, so treat it as background.\n\n**Where guidance differs.** US and European guidance describe LDL cholesterol treatment differently. US guidance is framed around statin intensity and percentage reduction, while European guidance sets explicit LDL goals and adds other drugs when a statin alone falls short. \"At goal\" means different things under each, so ask which framework your clinician uses.\n\n**What the sources don't cover.** They don't give evidence grades for each recommendation, don't name specific angina drugs, and don't give doses. Ask your clinician or a cardiology service for those details.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
